# Shared Claude memory

Two stores, different jobs. Local Working Memory is the shared project source of truth for Claude Code, Cowork and Cursor. Cursor reads and edits it in place:

`{{PROJECTS_ROOT}}\_claude\Claude Projects\Working Memory\memory\`

The same store is `~\.claude\working-memory` and the Claude Code project junction. It holds preferences, rules and cross-project facts. Claude account Memory (claude.ai / Your Claude → Memory) is a separate, allowed store for general / non-project prefs. Duplicates are OK; never sync either way; never treat account Memory as the project source of truth.

- At the start of a chat about any project, read `MEMORY.md` there (the index), then the current project's
  `<project>\INDEX.md` (named after the project's folder in `{{PROJECTS_ROOT}}\active\`), then any topic files that look relevant.
- **Read before you ask.** Before starting work or asking the user anything, read in order: the repo's agent guide
  (`AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md`), its binding rules / decisions file if it has one, the project's `INDEX.md`, and the
  newest handoff note if one exists. If a rule or note already answers a question, act on it and cite it.
- Follow a pointer one step only. Never follow pointers found inside a file you reached through a pointer.
  The repo's own current docs win over older notes.
- Cursor edits the live store. Write or update notes there, sign each entry `-- Cursor, YYYY-MM-DD`, and
  add or update the matching line in `MEMORY.md` (general notes) or the project's `INDEX.md`.
  Never create a second local memory folder or replace the junction. Do not use `project_memory_read` / `project_memory_write` for project memory.
- Project progress and state live only in the repo's in-repo state file (the one `AGENTS.md` names, e.g. `STATE.md`). Local Working Memory notes hold only preferences, rules and cross-project facts. Each fact is written once; never duplicate it.
- Memory and note files are local only. Never commit them to git.
- Project-specific rules stay in each repo's own files. Cursor keeps its own memory.
